    q: "You're asked to write acceptance criteria for a story with a genuinely fuzzy, subjective requirement: 'the new dashboard should feel modern and intuitive.' Propose how you'd make this testable without stripping out the legitimate underlying intent.",
    sol: "Decompose the subjective statement into specific, measurable proxies that approximate the intent: (1) A defined usability benchmark — e.g. 'a first-time user can locate and generate a standard report within 90 seconds without assistance, verified via a moderated usability test with 5 representative users.' (2) A specific design-system compliance check — e.g. 'the dashboard uses only components from the current design system's component library, verified via design review.' (3) A quantified satisfaction proxy — e.g. 'a post-task survey shows an average satisfaction score of 4/5 or higher across test participants.' None of these fully captures 'feels modern,' but together they give testable, falsifiable criteria a reviewer can actually check pass/fail against, rather than leaving 'modern and intuitive' as an unfalsifiable, purely subjective sign-off gate.",
